{
  "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
  "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This case raises the intersection of two similar doctrines of the same vintage that point in exactly opposite directions: The first, retroactivity; and the second, the good faith exception. This Court should reverse and hold that the good faith exception does not apply to reliance on precedent, and instead, that should be governed by the retroactivity principles of Griffith v. Kentucky."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Excuse me. You lose me in the argument because it doesn't seem to me that we -- if we did apply the good faith rule, we would be denying retroactive effect to the altered judgment. It continues to apply to matters beforehand, but it's a totally different question whether, assuming it is retroactive, the good faith exception to the exclusionary rule applies. It's a -- it's a separate question from retroactivity, it seems to me."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It's not applying existing law, because the effect of what is labeled the good faith exception in this setting is actually to apply the prior decision; that is, to have all of the substance and effect of a retroactivity decision."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yes, but just to pursue Justice Scalia's point: The good faith rule is itself a rule that's intact, that's a precedent, that was on the books, and the good faith rule qualifies the exclusionary rule. So why aren't we just following the good faith rule here?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The government is using a label of good faith for what is essentially a retroactivity argument."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I understand your argument with respect to decisions of this Court, and I think that's your strongest argument, but would you concede that that argument does not apply when the precedent on which the police are relying is a decision of one of the courts of appeals or a State supreme court Fourth Amendment decision?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Eleventh Circuit had not actually expanded upon Belton; it had merely had relatively routine applications of Belton."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Kerr, the line that has been suggested by Justice Scalia and Justice Kennedy, that is exactly the line that the Eleventh Circuit took; is it not the case? I mean, Judge Kravitch said: Yes, the Fourth Amendment was violated, so to that extent we're following retroactivity precedent. But the remedy is something different."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0000-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I'm not arguing that the exclusionary rule is available in every case when the Court overturns its precedent, merely that it is a remedy in some cases, and I think it's actually a necessary cost."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0000-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 13,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Kerr, let's say I'm concerned about the kinds of issues that you're raise, the incentives on defense counsel and also the oddity of the Court deciding a case in which it's absolutely not possible to grant a remedy. Wouldn't the solution to that be not the rule that you are advocating, but instead a much more limited rule which said that in that case the exclusionary rule applied, but not in any other case, not in any case on direct appeal?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I understand your position on that. Now, getting to my question, is it really worth it for us to have litigation over the difference between zero and remote in determining what rule we should apply in this case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 0.92,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0001-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Court has not applied the good faith exception in this setting."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Michael R. Dreeben (respondent counsel)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Because the exclusionary rule has severe consequences for the truth-seeking function of a criminal trial, this Court has restricted its application to those situations in which it's necessary to further the deterrent purpose of the exclusionary rule, namely to shape police conduct."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You've just raised the question of qualified immunity and the similarities. There is one problem that I have with the regime that you are suggesting, which is that because laws differ among circuits, now we've created a regime where we say if the law was clear or if there was law on this issue in your circuit, and the police officer relied on it, the exclusionary rule won't apply; but if the law was uncertain or not clear in another circuit, then the exclusionary rule does apply."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, particularly in light of that, what is wrong with the suggestion that Justice Kagan raised in one of her questions about retaining an exception to the good faith exception for the situation, for the defendant whose case comes up here and results in the overruling of one of this Court's precedents?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So I guess we would -- we would just grant the first and deny all the rest, even though they're raising the same issue?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Dreeben",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0002-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the results of my research disclose only one case in which a court of appeals overruled its precedent to favor a defendant, and that occurred in 1987 in the Fifth Circuit, and the Fifth Circuit adopted a good faith exception precisely like the one that the government is urging the court to adopt today and declined to suppress the evidence for that reason."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Orin S. Kerr)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would like to start with the costs -- or return to the costs here of the exclusionary rule. First, the plain error doctrine is going to largely address the concerns that the government has in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-11328",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1812768977ea14d58bdea56b6e4475e435d508ad1553b04fd718b06bc8f902d1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1812768977ea-0002-0007",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
